Title: Promotion marks the project copy dead in the form the walker reads
Created: 2026-09-10
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-09-10T15:50Z claude created

--- body ---

## Context

Promotion is meant to leave a dead marker behind in the project directory. The
skill writes a stub headed `# PROMOTED`; the walker that decides what is live
recognises only `# DELETED`. Five stubs are therefore still counted as live
bodies, and they inflate the per-slug frequency count that decides the next
promotion — so the defect feeds itself.

Design reference: defect 1 in `docs/2026-09-10-dragon-memory-design.md`.

## Relevant files

- `skills/dream/provenance.py` — the `live_bodies` walker; the head check is at
  the `startswith("# DELETED")` line
- `skills/dream/SKILL.md` — the promotion step that writes the `# PROMOTED` stub

## Actions

1. Choose one marker vocabulary. Two spellings for one state is the defect;
   picking either and applying it everywhere is the fix.
2. Make writer and reader agree, in the same commit.
3. Migrate the five existing `# PROMOTED` stubs to the surviving form.

## Test

Red first: assert that a body whose head carries the promotion marker is not
counted live. It fails today for all five.

## Verification

- [ ] `grep -rl '^# PROMOTED' projects/*/memory/*.md` returns nothing, or the
      walker counts what it returns as dead
- [ ] The live-body count drops by exactly five
- [ ] A promotion run leaves no body counted live for the promoted slug

## Invariants

- No body is deleted. A stub is a marker, not a removal.
- The provenance coverage gate stays shut.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] One marker value, not two, across writer and reader
- [ ] The five named slugs are counted dead — assert on the slugs, not on
      "the count drops by five", which races with any session writing memory
      before this merges
- [ ] The new marker is added to the coverage test's positive control
- [ ] A test asserts a promoted entry has no body counted live
